\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Orthogonal projections from a positive pairing}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Let #{V} be finite-dimensional over an ordered field #{F}, with
the positive symmetric pairing #{h} of the
\vocabk{Loewner comparison}{fdip-00K1}. For every subspace #{K},
the restriction #{h_K} is a
\vocabk{perfect symmetric pairing}{fdip-001O}. If #{i_K:K\to V}
is inclusion, there is a unique linear map #{p_K:V\to K} such that
##{h_K(k,p_Kv)=h(i_Kk,v)\qquad(k\in K,\ v\in V).}
It satisfies #{p_Ki_K=I_K}, and
##{V=i_K(K)\oplus K^\perp,\qquad
 K^\perp=\{v:h(i_Kk,v)=0\text{ for every }k\in K\}.}
For the adjoints defined by these pairings,
#{i_K^\dagger=p_K} and #{p_K^\dagger=i_K}.}

\proof{
\p{The currying map
#{\Phi_K:K\to K^\vee}, #{\Phi_K(y)(k)=h_K(k,y)}, is linear.
If #{\Phi_K(y)=0}, then #{h_K(y,y)=0}, so #{y=0}.
The dual and #{K} have the same finite dimension; hence this
injective map is bijective. Thus #{h_K} is perfect.}
\p{For each #{v}, represent the functional #{k\mapsto h(i_Kk,v)}
by its unique vector #{p_Kv}. Bilinearity and uniqueness give
linearity of #{p_K}. For #{v=i_Ky}, that representative is #{y},
so #{p_Ki_K=I_K}. The defining equation gives
#{v-i_Kp_Kv\in K^\perp}. An element of #{i_K(K)\cap K^\perp}
pairs to zero with itself and therefore vanishes. This proves the
direct sum and uniqueness of the projection. Applying the same
argument with #{K=V} also proves perfection of #{h}. The displayed
pairing identity and \ref{fdip-001P} then give
#{i_K^\dagger=p_K}; double adjunction gives the other identity.}
\p{When #{K=0}, the projection is zero and #{K^\perp=V}; when
#{K=V}, it is the identity and #{K^\perp=0}. The argument uses
finite-dimensional duality over #{F}, so it requires no square
roots or orthonormal basis.}
}

\p{The orthogonal-complement ingredient is formalized in
\leanref{LinearMap.BilinForm.isCompl_orthogonal_of_restrict_nondegenerate}:
a reflexive form over a field, a finite-dimensional ambient space
and a nondegenerate restriction give complementary subspaces.
The proof above establishes that restriction hypothesis from
positivity and constructs the projection with its adjoint identities.}
